(* --- Paragraphs --- *)
(* A VISIBLE block (heading, quote, table, fence, thematic break,
   admonition/div) interrupts a paragraph with no blank line before it
   (Markdown-like); invisible constructs (reference definitions, comments,
   block-attribute lines) interrupt too. A LIST marker (bullet OR ordered) is
   the exception: it does NOT interrupt -- a list needs a blank line before it
   (symmetric, Djot-like; see §10). This is the semantic constraint
   PART 9 §10, not a context-free one. A paragraph is terminated by a blank
   line, an interrupting block (§10), or end of file -- the optional trailing
   blank_line here is the paragraph's own terminator, not a precondition for
   the NEXT block.
   NO TRAILING WHITESPACE -- NORMATIVE. A `whitespace` run at the END of a
   CONTENT LINE is DROPPED. It does not reach the output, and it is not
   content.

   The rule is the project's, not this production's, and it is stated here
   because this is where it was first written down too narrowly:

     "trailing (invisible and bad) whitespace is the one important rule we
      have: no such thing."

   So it holds for EVERY content line, not only for a paragraph's final one.
   `abc<SP>` renders <p>abc</p>; so does `abc<SP>` followed by `def`, where the
   run sits before a SOFT BREAK; and so do a heading, a list item, a block
   quote line, a definition term or description, a footnote body line, a table
   caption and a line-block line.

   THE RUN IS `whitespace` -- `' ' | '\t'` -- the same two-character terminal
   `blank_line = {whitespace}` and `indent` take (PART 1; carve#890). Every
   other character is CONTENT and survives, however invisible: a no-break
   space, a zero-width space, a BYTE ORDER MARK, an en quad, an ideographic
   space, a form feed and a vertical tab all reach the output. That is what
   makes the rule simple rather than a list, and it is why U+FEFF was a red
   herring in the shape that raised this: in `<SP>U+FEFF<SP>` the BOM is
   content and what is dropped is the trailing SPACE.

   WHERE IT DOES NOT REACH. Verbatim content keeps its bytes: a fenced code
   block's body and a raw block's body are not content LINES in this sense,
   they are the block's payload. Whitespace INSIDE a construct is not trailing
   either -- `x<SP>` inside a code span, a literal inline or a table cell ends
   at the construct's delimiter, not at the line's end, and the line-break
   backslash likewise leaves the run before it interior.

   THE LINE BLOCK IS NOT AN EXCEPTION, in the order that matters. PART 9 §23's
   MEDIAL GAPS rule converts an inner or trailing run of TWO OR MORE columns
   into N\P CONTENT, and content is not whitespace, so this rule never reaches
   it. What it does reach is §23's one-column case, which that clause leaves
   as an ordinary collapsible space -- and a lone space cannot serve the
   purpose §23 gives it, letting a long line wrap between words, at the end of
   a line.

   (Carve has no two-trailing-space hard break -- a hard break is the backslash
   form, `\` + newline; see PART 3. This rule is why: a document cannot carry
   meaning in whitespace nothing can see.) Pinned by corpus
   104-paragraph-trailing-whitespace and 268. *)
paragraph = inline_content+, [blank_line] ;

